            <ArticleTitle>Usefulness of omalizumab in ten patients with severe occupational asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>813-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/all.12149</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The management of severe occupational asthma (OA) remains problematic and new alternative treatments providing better disease control are required, ideally enabling affected individuals to remain in their job.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ten patients with severe uncontrolled OA were treated with the monoclonal anti-IgE antibody omalizumab. In six cases the causative agent was a high molecular weight (HMW) compound and in four cases it was a low molecular weight (LMW) chemical. All of the patients had well documented OA despite workplace adjustments.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During treatment, nine patients exhibited a lower rate of asthma exacerbations and used less oral or inhaled corticosteroids. Seven patients were able to continue working at the same workplace as before treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We have demonstrated that omalizumab is a potential treatment for severe uncontrolled OA and enabled seven of the ten patients in the study to remain in their job.</AbstractText>
